__d(function(e,t,i,s,o,n,a){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"English",textbook:"A Pact with the Sun",chapter_number:7,chapter_title:"The Wonder Called Sleep",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What fraction of our lives do we spend sleeping?",back:"We spend approximately one-third of our lives sleeping. For a person who lives 75 years, that is about 25 years spent asleep \u2014 showing how essential sleep is to life.",memory_hook:"Life = 3 parts: Work, Play, Sleep. Sleep gets a full one-third share \u2014 it's not laziness, it's biology."},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the two main types of sleep?",back:"The two main types of sleep are NREM (Non-Rapid Eye Movement) sleep and REM (Rapid Eye Movement) sleep. They alternate in cycles throughout the night, each playing a different but essential role.",memory_hook:"N and R: NREM = body kNits itself back together. REM = bRain Encodes Memories."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does NREM stand for, and what happens during it?",back:"NREM stands for Non-Rapid Eye Movement. During NREM sleep, the body is in deep rest \u2014 brain waves slow down, growth hormones are released, body tissues are repaired, muscles grow, and the immune system is strengthened.",memory_hook:"NREM = Night Repair and Equipment Maintenance. Your body is a factory running the night shift."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does REM stand for, and what happens during it?",back:"REM stands for Rapid Eye Movement. During REM sleep, the brain is highly active, the eyes move rapidly under closed eyelids, and we dream. This is the phase when new memories and learning are consolidated into long-term storage.",memory_hook:"REM = Really Exciting Mind-activity. Your brain is basically awake \u2014 replaying and saving the day's files."},{id:"fc05",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How long does one complete sleep cycle last, and how many cycles do we need per night?",back:"One complete sleep cycle lasts approximately 90 minutes, moving through NREM stages and then REM sleep. A healthy night's sleep involves 4 to 5 complete cycles \u2014 meaning 6 to 7.5 hours at minimum for adults, and more for children.",memory_hook:"90 minutes = 1 cycle. Think of it as a school period \u2014 your brain needs 4 to 5 'periods' of sleep each night."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How many hours of sleep do students aged 10\u201312 need per night?",back:"Students aged 10 to 12 need 9 to 11 hours of sleep per night. This is more than adults (who need 7 to 8 hours) because children's brains and bodies are actively growing and developing.",memory_hook:"Growing needs more charging. A child's body is like a bigger phone battery \u2014 it needs longer to charge fully."},{id:"fc07",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is 'sleep deprivation'? Name three of its effects.",back:"Sleep deprivation means not getting enough sleep regularly. Three major effects are: (1) poor concentration \u2014 difficulty paying attention in class; (2) memory loss \u2014 lessons are forgotten quickly; (3) irritability \u2014 mood swings and emotional instability. Long-term effects include weakened immunity and health problems.",memory_hook:"TIRED: Thinking slows, Irritability rises, Remembering fails, Energy crashes, Disease risk rises."},{id:"fc08",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is REM sleep especially important for students who are learning new things?",back:"During REM sleep, the brain consolidates new information \u2014 it transfers what was learned during the day from short-term (temporary) memory to long-term (permanent) memory. A student who sleeps well after studying remembers the material far better than one who skipped sleep.",memory_hook:"REM = Save button for your brain. No REM = no save. You studied but the file was never stored."},{id:"fc09",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is sleep considered an 'active' biological process rather than mere rest?",back:"During sleep, the body and brain are extremely busy: growth hormones repair tissues and build muscle, the immune system produces infection-fighting proteins, the brain clears out toxins through the glymphatic system, and new memories are consolidated. Far from being passive, sleep is the body's most productive maintenance period.",memory_hook:"Sleep = Service Centre. Every night your body goes into the garage for oil changes, repairs, and software updates."},{id:"fc10",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the 'circadian rhythm'?",back:"The circadian rhythm is the body's internal 24-hour biological clock that regulates when we feel awake and when we feel sleepy. It is set by light and darkness \u2014 daylight keeps us alert, and darkness triggers the release of melatonin, the sleep hormone. Screens at night disrupt this clock.",memory_hook:"Circa = around, dian = day. Circadian = around-the-day clock. Your body's built-in sunrise-sunset timer."},{id:"fc11",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the glymphatic system and when does it work?",back:"The glymphatic system is a waste-clearance system in the brain that flushes out harmful toxins accumulated during waking hours. It is most active during sleep, particularly deep NREM sleep. Without adequate sleep, these toxins build up and can damage brain health over time.",memory_hook:"Glymphatic = Gutter system of the brain. While you sleep, the brain empties its rubbish bins."},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why does using screens (phone, TV, tablet) before bed reduce sleep quality?",back:"Screens emit blue light, which suppresses melatonin \u2014 the hormone that tells the body it is time to sleep. This tricks the brain into thinking it is still daytime, delaying sleep onset and disrupting the circadian rhythm. The result is less total sleep and fewer complete REM cycles.",memory_hook:"Blue light = brain thinks it's blue-sky daytime. No melatonin = no sleepiness = less REM = less memory."},{id:"fc13",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why can't 'catching up' on sleep during weekends fully replace weekday sleep loss?",back:"Research shows that while weekend sleep provides some recovery, the cognitive damage from weekday sleep deprivation \u2014 particularly to memory consolidation and concentration \u2014 cannot be fully reversed. REM sleep lost on Monday night cannot be fully recovered on Saturday. Consistent daily sleep is far more effective.",memory_hook:"Sleep debt is not like money debt \u2014 you can't fully repay it in a lump sum on weekends."},{id:"fc14",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Which Bengaluru institution studies sleep disorders and brain health in India?",back:"NIMHANS \u2014 the National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences \u2014 located in Bengaluru, Karnataka, is one of India's premier institutions studying brain health, sleep disorders, and mental health. Their research shows that sleep-deprived Indian students perform significantly worse on memory tests.",memory_hook:"NIMHANS is to sleep research what IISc is to science \u2014 Bengaluru's brain centre studies your sleeping brain."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"You have a science exam tomorrow. Is it better to study until 2 a.m. or to study until 10 p.m. and sleep 8 hours? Why?",back:"Sleep until 10 p.m. and sleep 8 hours is far better. Studying until 2 a.m. means only 4 hours of sleep \u2014 the brain will have insufficient REM sleep to consolidate what was studied. You will feel tired, memory will be impaired, and exam performance will suffer. 8 hours of sleep ensures full memory consolidation.",memory_hook:"Study smart, not long. 6 hours study + 8 hours sleep beats 9 hours study + 5 hours sleep \u2014 every time."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What role do growth hormones play during sleep, and why is this especially important for children?",back:"During deep NREM sleep, the pituitary gland releases growth hormones that repair damaged tissues, build and strengthen muscles, and support physical growth. This is especially important for children and teenagers whose bodies are actively growing. Insufficient sleep directly stunts growth and slows physical development.",memory_hook:"Sleep = Grow mode ON. The body only presses the GROW button during deep sleep \u2014 not while you're awake."},{id:"fc17",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does sleep strengthen the immune system?",back:"During sleep, the immune system produces cytokines \u2014 proteins that help fight infection, inflammation, and stress. Sleep deprivation reduces cytokine production, making the body more vulnerable to viruses, bacteria, and illness. Well-rested students get fewer colds and recover faster when ill.",memory_hook:"Sleep = Soldier Factory. While you rest, your immune system is manufacturing tiny soldiers (cytokines) to protect you."},{id:"fc18",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is insomnia?",back:"Insomnia is a sleep disorder in which a person has persistent difficulty falling asleep or staying asleep, resulting in poor sleep quality and tiredness during the day. It can be caused by stress, anxiety, irregular schedules, or excessive screen time before bed.",memory_hook:"Insomnia = In-som-nia = 'I have NO sleep.' The 'in' prefix means absence \u2014 absence of restful sleep."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"An athlete trains hard every day. How does sleep help their performance and recovery?",back:"During deep NREM sleep, growth hormones repair the micro-tears in muscles caused by training, making them stronger. REM sleep consolidates motor-skill memory \u2014 helping the brain 'practise' movements learned during the day. Athletes who sleep 8\u20139 hours recover faster, are less injury-prone, and perform better than those who sleep 5\u20136 hours.",memory_hook:"Sleep = Training Session 2. The body physically improves during sleep, not only during training."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"List three practical habits a student can follow to improve their sleep quality.",back:"(1) Maintain a consistent sleep schedule \u2014 same bedtime and wake time every day, even weekends. (2) Avoid screens for at least 30\u201360 minutes before bed to protect melatonin production. (3) Sleep in a cool, dark, quiet room to support the body's natural transition into deep NREM sleep.",memory_hook:"Three Sleep Rules: CONSISTENT time, NO screens before bed, DARK quiet room. CND = Clean Night's rest Discipline."}]}},2945,[]);